---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import EndocrineSim from '../../simulators/endocrine/EndocrineSim.astro';
import { endocrineQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/endocrine';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What the endocrine system does' },
  { id: 'glands', label: 'The major glands' },
  { id: 'how', label: 'How hormones work' },
  { id: 'feedback', label: 'Negative feedback: the thyroid' },
  { id: 'glucose', label: 'Controlling blood glucose' },
  { id: 'stress', label: 'Stress, growth and reproduction' },
  { id: 'relevance', label: 'Hormones and health' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Betts, J. G., et al. (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e, Chapter 17: The Endocrine System. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/17-introduction' },
  { text: 'Hall, J. E., & Hall, M. E. (2020). Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology (14th ed.), Chapters 75–80. Elsevier.' },
  { text: 'Dietrich, J. W., Landgrafe, G., & Fotiadou, E. H. (2012). TSH and thyrotropic agonists: key actors in thyroid homeostasis. Journal of Thyroid Research, 2012, 351864.' },
  { text: 'Bergman, R. N. (1989). Toward physiological understanding of glucose tolerance: minimal-model approach. Diabetes, 38(12), 1512–1527.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="anatomy-physiology"
  topic="endocrine-system"
  description="Interactive endocrine system simulator: explore the major glands and their hormones, run the thyroid negative feedback loop, and control blood glucose with insulin and glucagon."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={endocrineQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Your body runs on chemical messages as well as electrical ones. Hormones released by small glands travel in the
      blood and reach almost every cell, adjusting growth, energy use, stress responses and reproduction. Explore the
      glands, change hormone levels, and see how feedback loops keep the body in balance.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <EndocrineSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Glands &amp; hormones:</strong> choose or click a gland. Watch its hormone travel in the blood to its target organs, then change the <strong>hormone level</strong> and read the effects.</li>
      <li><strong>Thyroid feedback:</strong> press <strong>Start</strong> and change the <strong>situation</strong> or add <strong>tablets</strong>. Watch TRH, TSH and T4 respond on the graph, and the thyroid swell in iodine deficiency.</li>
      <li><strong>Blood glucose:</strong> press <strong>Eat a meal</strong> or <strong>Exercise</strong> and watch glucose, insulin and glucagon. Switch to type 1 or type 2 diabetes and compare; try <strong>Inject insulin</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what">What the endocrine system does</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>endocrine system</strong> is a set of glands that release <strong>hormones</strong>, chemical messengers,
    into the blood. Hormones regulate long-term processes: <strong>growth</strong>, <strong>metabolism</strong>,
    <strong>stress responses</strong>, <strong>reproduction</strong>, and <strong>homeostasis</strong> (keeping
    conditions such as blood glucose, water and salt levels steady). It works alongside the nervous system: nerves send
    fast, targeted signals, while hormones act more slowly but often for longer and over the whole body.
  </p>

  <h2 id="glands">The major glands</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Gland</th><th>Main hormones</th><th>Main effects</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Hypothalamus</td><td>Releasing hormones, ADH</td><td>Controls the pituitary; links brain and hormones</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pituitary</td><td>Growth hormone, TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH, ADH</td><td>Growth; controls other glands; water balance</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Thyroid</td><td>Thyroxine (T4), T3</td><td>Metabolic rate, growth and development</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Adrenal glands</td><td>Adrenaline, cortisol, aldosterone</td><td>Fight or flight, stress, salt balance</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pancreas</td><td>Insulin, glucagon</td><td>Blood glucose control</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ovaries / testes</td><td>Oestrogen, progesterone / testosterone</td><td>Puberty and reproduction</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="how">How hormones work</h2>
  <p>
    Hormones travel everywhere in the blood, but only <strong>target cells</strong> with the matching
    <strong>receptor</strong> respond, like a key fitting a lock. Some hormones, such as adrenaline and insulin, bind to
    receptors on the cell surface and trigger rapid changes inside. Others, such as thyroid and steroid hormones, enter the
    cell and switch genes on or off, causing slower, longer-lasting effects.
  </p>

  <h2 id="feedback">Negative feedback: the thyroid</h2>
  <p>
    Most hormone levels are controlled by <strong>negative feedback</strong>. The hypothalamus releases
    <strong>TRH</strong>, which tells the pituitary to release <strong>TSH</strong>, which tells the thyroid to make
    <strong>thyroxine (T4)</strong>. T4 then reduces TRH and TSH. If T4 rises, the chain is turned down; if it falls, the
    chain is turned up. This explains thyroid problems: lack of iodine means the thyroid cannot make enough T4, so TSH
    stays high and the gland swells into a <strong>goitre</strong>; in an overactive thyroid, T4 is high and TSH almost
    disappears.
  </p>

  <h2 id="glucose">Controlling blood glucose</h2>
  <p>
    Cells need a steady supply of glucose, and the brain especially. After a meal, rising glucose triggers the pancreas
    to release <strong>insulin</strong>, which helps muscle, fat and liver cells take up glucose; the liver stores it as
    <strong>glycogen</strong>. Between meals, falling glucose triggers <strong>glucagon</strong>, which tells the liver to
    break glycogen down and release glucose. Together they keep glucose at about 4–7 mmol/L. In <strong>type 1
    diabetes</strong> the insulin-making cells are destroyed, so insulin must be injected; in <strong>type 2
    diabetes</strong> cells respond poorly to insulin.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stress">Stress, growth and reproduction</h2>
  <p>
    In an emergency the adrenal glands release <strong>adrenaline</strong> within seconds: heart rate and breathing
    increase and glucose is released for the muscles. Longer-term stress raises <strong>cortisol</strong>, which keeps
    glucose high but can weaken the immune system. <strong>Growth hormone</strong> from the pituitary drives growth in
    childhood. Sex hormones from the ovaries and testes cause puberty and control the menstrual cycle and sperm
    production.
  </p>

  <h2 id="relevance">Hormones and health</h2>
  <p>
    Many conditions involve hormones, including diabetes, thyroid disorders and growth disorders. Blood tests measure
    hormones such as TSH, T4 and glucose. Treatments often replace a missing hormone, such as insulin injections or
    thyroxine tablets, which is why understanding feedback matters: doses are adjusted using feedback signals such as
    TSH levels.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Hormone</dt>
    <dd>A chemical messenger made by a gland and carried in the blood to target organs.</dd>
    <dt>Target organ</dt>
    <dd>An organ whose cells have receptors for a particular hormone.</dd>
    <dt>Negative feedback</dt>
    <dd>A control system where a change triggers a response that reverses it.</dd>
    <dt>Homeostasis</dt>
    <dd>Keeping internal conditions within narrow limits.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Hormones only matter during puberty.”</strong> Hormones control body processes every minute of life.</li>
    <li><strong>“Each hormone affects every cell.”</strong> Only cells with the right receptors respond.</li>
    <li><strong>“More hormone is always better.”</strong> Too much is as harmful as too little; feedback keeps levels balanced.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <p>
      <strong>Educational model only.</strong> These are simplified models: real hormone systems involve many more
      hormones, feedback loops and differences between people. Values shown are illustrative, and this simulator cannot
      be used to diagnose or manage diabetes, thyroid conditions or any other condition, or to choose doses of medicine.
      Always follow the advice of a qualified healthcare professional. See our <a href="/disclaimer/">educational and
      medical disclaimer</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Endocrine glands release hormones into the blood; only target cells with receptors respond.</li>
    <li>The hypothalamus and pituitary control many other glands.</li>
    <li>Negative feedback keeps hormone levels steady, as in the thyroid axis.</li>
    <li>Insulin and glucagon keep blood glucose in a narrow range.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: thyroid levels are relative to normal and use a simple three-hormone feedback loop; the glucose model uses simplified rates tuned to typical textbook responses.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
